{"context":"The release lifecycle defined in ADR-003 describes how releases progress from creation to retirement. But a release is not a physical object — it is a logical entity that references something deployable. That deployable object is the Artifact.\n\nWithout a clearly defined Artifact concept, the following problems emerge:\n\n**Ambiguous deployment units.** Teams deploy different things — source code checked out on servers, git archives, container images, tarballs of unknown provenance. There is no single answer to the question \"what was actually deployed?\"\n\n**Mutability undermines verification.** If the thing being deployed can change after verification, the verification is meaningless. What was verified is not what runs in production. This is the root cause of many deployment incidents.\n\n**No s Source: ADR-004-artifact-architecture.md Date:2026-07-19 Status:Accepted.","decision":"Anvil adopts an immutable Artifact architecture.\n\nAn Artifact is a packaged, immutable representation of deployable application content, accompanied by identifying and verifying metadata.\n\nEvery release originates from exactly one Artifact. A release references an Artifact; it is not equivalent to one.\n\nThe Artifact is the canonical deployment unit. It is created once, verified once, and never modified.\n\nThe key architectural relationships are:\n\n- **Artifact** — the immutable package of deployable content\n- **Release** — a logical entity that references an Artifact and tracks lifecycle state\n- **Runtime** — the execution environment where a Release is activated\n\nApplications MUST NOT activate Artifacts directly. Activation always occurs through a Release.","consequences":"### 9.1 Positive Consequences\n\n**Deterministic deployments.** Because Artifacts are immutable, the content deployed to every environment is known and fixed. There is no ambiguity about what was deployed.\n\n**Reliable verification.** Verification results remain valid for the Artifact's entire lifetime. What was verified is what gets deployed, even weeks or months later.\n\n**Safe rollback.** Rollback targets are immutable Artifacts that were previously verified. Rolling back restores a known-good state without re-verification.\n\n**Portable releases.** The same Artifact can be promoted across environments without rebuilding. This eliminates the risk of environment-specific build artifacts.\n\n**Consistent deployment model.** Every project, framework, and environment uses the same Artifact model. T","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n### 10.1 Mutable Deployment Packages\n\n**Description:** The deployment unit can be modified after creation. Patches, configuration overrides, and environment adaptations are applied directly to the deployed content.\n\n**Rejected because:** Mutability invalidates verification. What was verified is not what runs in production. Rollback becomes unreliable because the \"previous\" deployment may have been patched and is no longer in a known-good state. Mutable deployment packages are the sou"}